# Engineering Specifications

Use the canonical repository-local Router. This Claude adapter provides native
Skill discovery, but Specification activation and enforcement are explicit CLI
steps; it does not claim lifecycle Hook enforcement.

Ordinary code explanation and navigation do not require this workflow.
For changes or formal review, choose stable session and turn identifiers and run:

```bash
python3 .repo-foundry/engineering-specs/spec_router.py --repo . \
  begin --adapter-id claude --session-id <session> --turn-id <turn> \
  --prompt <task-summary>
```

The `begin` result reports adaptive/strict profile and Explore/Build/Governed
mode. Adaptive Explore permits bounded reversible local work without a receipt.
Promote before bounded production or governed-risk work:

```bash
python3 .repo-foundry/engineering-specs/spec_router.py --repo . \
  classify --adapter-id claude --session-id <session> --turn-id <turn> \
  --mode <build|governed> --reason <risk-reason>
```

In Build/Governed, read each candidate's Applicability and Requirement Activation
cards before excluding it. Request one candidate Spec per call, then evaluate both
levels against task and code. Keep uncertain conditions or conflicts explicit;
resolve them before affected high-risk operations. Record exact direct IDs with reasons:

```bash
python3 .repo-foundry/engineering-specs/spec_router.py --repo . \
  candidates --path <path>
python3 .repo-foundry/engineering-specs/spec_router.py --repo . \
  requirements --path <path> --spec <candidate-spec-id>
python3 .repo-foundry/engineering-specs/spec_router.py --repo . \
  activate --adapter-id claude --session-id <session> --turn-id <turn> \
  --path <path> --spec <applicable-spec-id> \
  --requirement <ID> --because "<ID>=<task-specific reason>"
```

Cover all candidate batches; `applicable_specs` echoes requested entries, not a
verdict. Split batches or adjust the card budget without dropping rules; if one
Spec still cannot be reviewed completely, report the gap. Whole-Spec entries
without cards retain source review. No persistent decision table is required.
Repeat the flags for multiple selections. Use `--whole-spec` plus
`--whole-spec-reason` only for an indexed legacy fallback, migration, or broad
audit. If none applies, use `--none` with a reason. After compaction, run
`rehydrate` for the active identity. Never infer none from an empty candidate
list and never summarize or truncate normative text to fit a budget. Raising
the default capsule budget requires `--capsule-budget-reason`.

Before the **first** Build/Governed edit, review all applicable
Requirement cards for identifiers, common paths, control flow, errors and
tests. Enumerate exact intended source/test files, not just a glob. Without
Hooks, use separate, sequential tool calls for activation, `rehydrate`, and
preflight; do not combine or parallelize them in one outer tool call. Deliver
original text in its own response without unrelated output. Account for context
and framing as well as capsule bytes; byte and host output budgets differ.
Inspect the actual model-visible response for truncation or omitted content.
Saved output, a digest or an epoch flag alone is not complete-delivery evidence.
Recover exact segments of the same capsule at one digest/epoch, covering the
full ordered range without gaps; do not rehydrate between segments. Never
summarize or shrink the applicable set to fit. If complete delivery cannot be
established, stop affected edits and report UNVERIFIED. Then run:

```bash
python3 .repo-foundry/engineering-specs/spec_router.py --repo . \
  preflight --adapter-id claude --session-id <session> --turn-id <turn> \
  --path <exact-source> --path <exact-test> \
  [--expect-requirement <independently-identified-ID> ...]
```

This is a read-only snapshot gate, not an applicability inference or native
write block. The delivery flag checks Router bookkeeping, not outer responses;
Agent-supplied expected IDs do not prove selection completeness. An activation
after editing cannot certify earlier compliance.
Review new names and branches before later changes. If required canonical
verification cannot run (including offline dependency failures), report
UNVERIFIED/incomplete rather than PASS. Do not substitute source review for
required no-effect integration evidence.

Before Build/Governed completion, audit the receipt and handoff:

```bash
python3 .repo-foundry/engineering-specs/spec_router.py --repo . \
  evidence --adapter-id claude --session-id <session> --turn-id <turn>
python3 .repo-foundry/engineering-specs/spec_router.py --repo . \
  audit --adapter-id claude --session-id <session> --turn-id <turn> \
  --message-file <handoff-file>
```

Explore reports outcome, verification, and unresolved risk in ordinary prose.
Separate implementation results, executed checks, activation/delivery observations
and gaps; audit success does not certify code compliance.
The evidence export carries source-owned published levels and an Advisory
effective level. It is activation evidence, not a finding-lifecycle claim.
Build/Governed responses contain `Activated specifications:`,
`Activated requirements:`, `Verification:`, `Exceptions:`, and
`Compatibility or migration:`. Report a missing Router as an exception rather
than claiming that no Specification applies. Never use Explore to bypass
authority, destructive/external action, security, data, or integrity boundaries.
